THREE LITERAL AXES, each reverted, all measured with `python tools/gdl/probe.py game/movie/movieplayer fn_800D91B4 --raw` at head c7b741799.

BASELINE: real 136, insns T136/O136, multiset 0t, RAW WORDS = 73 of 136, DECODE REGFIELD-ONLY 63 / OPCODE 10. The 10 opcode divergences all lie in the window [0xd0,0x100); the other 63 words are a whole-function callee-saved recolour with ZERO opcode divergence (verified by reading 0x120:0x220 aligned -- every row is `=` or `~`).

AXIS 1 -- STATEMENT ORDER (the mandated lever). In the `count > 256` block, the six independent statements were reordered from
        bits = *indices;
        bitBytes = indices;
        indices += (state->width / 2 * state->height / 2) / 8;
        bitCount = 0;
        y = 0;
        bitBytes++;
to the order the TARGET's emission implies (bitBytes copy at +0xcc, the `lbz` for bits at +0xd0, `li` bitCount at +0xd4, the divide chain from +0xd8, `li` y interleaved at +0xe4, `addi` bitBytes at +0x100):
        bitBytes = indices;
        bits = *indices;
        bitCount = 0;
        indices += (state->width / 2 * state->height / 2) / 8;
        y = 0;
        bitBytes++;
RESULT: NEUTRAL-IDENTICAL -- the object bytes did not move. Proof line printed for citation:
  NEUTRAL-IDENTICAL-PROOF unit=game/movie/movieplayer fn=fn_800D91B4 bytes=34f4b0cb406c real=136 insns=T136/O136 multiset=0t head=c7b741799
CATEGORICAL, and decisive at one probe: the front end normalises these six statements into one expression DAG before the scheduler chooses, so no further SPELLING of the same construct can reach the decision point either.

AXIS 2 -- FUNCTION-SCOPE DECLARATION ORDER. `int count;` moved from first to last of the six locals:
    u8* palette;
    int rowStride;
    u8* indices;
    int i;
    int offset;
    int count;
(chosen because the target's callee-saved numbering descends palette r31, rowStride r30, indices r29, i r26, offset r24, count r21 -- i.e. count LAST -- against ours palette r31, offset r30, indices r29, i r28, count r27.)
RESULT: NEUTRAL, and the axis IS LIVE -- it reached codegen and renumbered four local webs (count r27->r24, i r28->r27, offset r30->r28, entry r24->r30). But RAW WORDS stayed at 73 and the DECODE split stayed at REGFIELD-ONLY 63 / OPCODE 10, and the three PARAMETER homes did not move by one register:
  0x10  T `mr r22,r3`      O `mr r25,r3`      (state)
  0x14  T `addi r27,r6,0`  O `addi r21,r6,0`  (bitmap)
  0x18  T `addi r23,r7,0`  O `addi r26,r7,0`  (dst)
Those three webs alone account for 25 of the 63 register-field words (state at 0x28,0x94,0x98,0xb8,0xc8,0xdc,0x170,0x184,0x1dc,0x1f0,0x1fc,0x208 and the home; dst at 0xb0,0x128,0x13c,0x168,0x17c,0x1a8,0x1c4,0x1d4,0x1e8 and the home; bitmap at 0x88 and the home). THIS IS THE FINDING THE AXIS BUYS: local declaration order reaches the LOCAL numbering and provably does not reach the PARAMETER homes, so the largest single component of the recolour is out of this lever's range.

AXIS 3 -- DECLARATION SCOPE. The three block-scope declarations of the `count > 256` arm (`s32 bits; u8* bitBytes; u8 bitCount;`) hoisted to function scope, block-scope copies deleted, statement order and function-scope order otherwise untouched.
RESULT: BYTE-IDENTICAL TO BASELINE -- a second fold-away. Verified by reading the object back rather than by trusting the verdict: `fnasm game/movie/movieplayer fn_800D91B4 0xc8:0x108 --diff --raw` reproduces the baseline window exactly (ours `d0: lwz r0,4(r25)`, `d4: li r24,0`, `e0: lbz r28,0(r29)`, `fc: li r23,0`) and `... 0x20:0x48 --diff --raw` reproduces the baseline entry exactly (`24: clrlwi r27,r3,16`, `38: li r28,0`, `3c: li r30,0`, `44: add r24,r31,r30`).

RAW WORDS across all three probes: 73 -> 73 -> 73 -> 73. DECODE split across all three: 63/10 unchanged in every probe. No form moved a single word of either component toward the target.
